Now I understand the test patterns. Note the existing test at line ~1268 asserts `aux.state === 'success'` — fix 4 will rename that to `verdict`, so I need to update this test (it's part of the PR's own tests; changing it is expected since the review thread asks for the rename).

Also note the important test at line 1247: "cite prefers the real validation run over a fresher SM side-run leg" — realRun is a push event (CI), legRun is a dispatch. With fix 2 scoring, realRun (push) is score 1, dynRun (quality.yml dispatch) is score 2. In that test, runs = [legRun(), dynRun(), realRun()]. Cite expected: runId 37925202998 (realRun, CI push, freshest at 11:56). With my scoring change: terminal candidates: dynRun (score 2, updated 11:12), realRun (score 1, 11:56). Score-2 tier first → dynRun wins → runId 37921773706 ≠ 37925202998. **This existing test would break!**

Hmm. Wait — is that the right outcome though? With the preference scoring, the machine's own validation dispatch (dynRun, quality.yml) would beat the fresher repo CI push run. Is that desirable per the reviewer? The reviewer says "dynamic PR-validation runs and repo CI still win over legs, and unrelated workflows only cite when nothing better exists." The scoring puts ciWorkflow dispatch matches above bare non-dispatch runs — that includes repo CI push runs. So yes, per the suggested design, the quality.yml dispatch beats a fresher `ci.yml` push run.

But does that conflict with the existing test's intent? The existing test asserts the freshest REAL run (full CI push) is the cite. Under the new preference, the test expectation must change (dynRun becomes the cite). Hmm, but wait — is that a good idea? On #1437, the real validations were full CI + dynamic PR-validation; both are "real". Either is honest discharge evidence. The reviewer's exact concern: an unrelated workflow (labeler/docs/lint) stealing the cite from the machine's green validation.

